##### Art. 107 Measures Depriving Other Rights

In the cases provided for in articles 103 to 105 of this Code, the Judge or Court may reasonably order, from the beginning or during the execution of the sentence, the imposition of compliance with one or more of the following measures, for a period not exceeding five years:

a) Submission to external treatment in medical centers;
b) Obligation to reside in a specified place;
c) Prohibition from residing in the place or territory designated. In this case, the subject shall be obliged to declare the domicile chosen and any changes that occur;
d) Prohibition from going to certain places or visiting establishments serving alcoholic beverages;
e) Family custody; the person subject to family custody shall be subject to the care and supervision of the designated family member who accepts the custody, who shall exercise it in relation to the supervising Judge and without detriment to the educational or work activities of the person in custody;
f) Submission to programs of a formative, cultural, educational, professional, sexual education and other similar nature.

For a period of up to ten years:
a) Deprivation of the license or permit to carry weapons;
b) Deprivation of the right to drive motor vehicles.

##### Art. 108 Foreigner with Illegal Entry or Stay

If the subject is a foreigner with illegal entry or stay in Nicaragua, the Judge or Court may order, after hearing the subject, expulsion from the national territory as a substitute for the custodial security measures applicable to him or her.

The person subject to this measure may not re-enter Nicaragua for a period not less than double the duration of the security measure that would be applicable, without exceeding ten years.

##### Art. 109 Habitual Offenders

For habitual offenders responsible for crimes punishable by imprisonment whose minimum limit exceeds six years, the Judge or Court shall impose, in addition to the corresponding penalty, a social therapy treatment for their reintegration for the period of their sentence.

For the purposes of this article, an offender is considered habitual who has been convicted of three or more crimes which, not having been cancelled from the registry, make presumable his or her inclination to commit crimes, according to express declaration by the Judge or Court, following the expert reports that may be necessary.

##### Art. 110 Confinement of Women

When the crime has been committed by women they must be confined in prisons intended exclusively for them, or in pavilions of penal establishments duly separated from the cells of men. These prisons and pavilions must be managed by female prison officials, in accordance with the law and regulations on the matter.

#### CHAPTER III

URGENT PROTECTION MEASURES

##### Art. 111

Urgent protection measures for victims of intrafamily or domestic violence
When the act or omission has been committed by a family member up to the fourth degree of consanguinity or second degree of affinity within the cohabiting family or in a stable de facto union, the judicial authority of the place where the act occurred, at the request of a party, may apply, as appropriate, the following protection measures:
a) Order the immediate abandonment of the home by the accused or defendant and, taking into account the victim's wishes, return the victim to the home from which he or she was removed by violence or intimidation;
b) Prohibit or restrict the presence of the reported person in the dwelling of the injured party within a minimum radius of one hundred fifty meters;
c) Order the reintegration of the injured person to the home from which he or she was removed by violence or intimidation;
d) Prohibit or limit the presence of the reported person at the workplace of the injured party within a minimum radius of one hundred fifty meters;
e) Guarantee the injured party medical, psychological or psychiatric care if necessary. The reported person shall undergo the same care if necessary for his or her rehabilitation and to prevent recidivism;
f) Order the bio-psycho-social examination of minors involved in acts of domestic or intrafamily violence and provide them with due care;
g) In case of reports of child abuse, the corresponding authority shall be requested to intervene through specialized agencies to conduct the investigation and provide support, protection, advice, counseling and respective follow-up;
h) The reported person must provide sufficient guarantees as determined by the Judge to compensate for possible damages caused to the injured party;

i) In case the victim is a minor or person with disabilities, the competent judicial authority may provisionally entrust protective custody to whomever it considers suitable for such function, if it was entrusted to the aggressor;
j) Prohibit all forms of harassment that disturb the peace of the injured party including electromagnetic or other means;
k) Order the confiscation of weapons from the reported person.

In the case of indigenous peoples of the Atlantic Coast, the measures shall be applied by the communal authority in accordance with customary law and current laws.

The Judge or Court may order the protection measures referred to in the preceding paragraphs upon becoming aware of the crimes. For their enforcement, it may request the assistance of the National Police.

The National Police and the Public Prosecutor's Office in the investigative stage shall request from the judicial authority the application of the protection measures referred to in this article on a preventive basis, for a period not exceeding ten days.

When the Public Prosecutor's Office decides not to exercise criminal action, the victim or his or her representative may request the criminal judge to apply or maintain the protection measures for the period it takes to resolve the respective appeals.

In case of non-compliance by the accused with the protection measures ordered by the judge, the judge shall proceed to apply a more severe measure, at the request of a party.

### TITLE V

OTHER ACCESSORY CONSEQUENCES OF CRIME

SOLE CHAPTER CONFISCATION AND OTHER CONSEQUENCES

##### Art. 112 Confiscation

Any penalty imposed for an intentional, negligent crime or misdemeanor shall entail the forfeiture of the effects arising therefrom or of property acquired with the value of such effects, of the instruments with which it was executed or had been intended for its execution, or of the profits arising from the criminal offense, whatever transformations they may undergo. Both shall be confiscated, unless they belong to a third party in good faith not responsible for the crime and who has acquired them legally.

The confiscated effects, instruments or profits shall be sold if they are of lawful commerce, applying the proceeds to cover the civil liabilities of the convicted person; if they are not, they shall be given the corresponding destination and, failing that, shall be rendered unusable. When they are firearms or weapons of war, they shall be placed at the disposal of the National Police or the Army of Nicaragua, as appropriate.

When the said effects and instruments are of lawful commerce and their value is not proportionate to the nature or gravity of the criminal offense, or the civil liabilities are completely satisfied, the Judge or Court may not decree confiscation, or decree it partially.

