##### Art. 239 Irregular appropriation

The following shall be punished with a fine of thirty to one hundred eighty days or community service of thirty to one hundred fifty days of two hours daily:

a) Whoever appropriates a lost thing belonging to another without complying with the requirements prescribed by Law;

b) Whoever appropriates a thing belonging to another into whose possession he has come as a consequence of an error or fortuitous event; and,

c) Whoever appropriates in whole or in part a discovered treasure, without delivering the portion that corresponds to the owner of the property, in accordance with the Law.

#### CHAPTER VII USURPATION

##### Art. 240 Usurpation of private domain

A penalty of six months to three years of imprisonment shall be imposed on whoever:

a) By violence, intimidation, deceit or abuse of trust dispossesses another, totally or partially, of the possession or holding of real property or of the exercise of a real right constituted over it, whether the dispossession occurs by invading the property, remaining on it or expelling the occupants;

b) To take possession of all or part of real property, alters its boundaries or limits; or

c) By violence or intimidation, disturbs the possession or holding of real property.

##### Art. 241 Usurpation of public or communal domain

The following shall be punished with imprisonment of one to three years:

a) Whoever, without authorization, permanently occupies land or space corresponding to streets, roads, gardens, parks, promenades or other places of public domain, or vacant lands or any other real property of the State, of the Autonomous Regions of the Atlantic Coast or of the Municipalities;

b) Whoever, without being legally authorized, exploits veins, deposits, mantles and other mineral deposits;

c) Whoever, making use of concessions granted by law for a specific purpose, has entered into possession of land and uses it for a purpose different from that authorized, or, after exploiting the respective forest, abandons said land without fulfilling the commitments acquired in relation to said exploitation;

d) Whoever occupies communal lands or lands belonging to indigenous communities;

e) Whoever by acts of force restricts, limits or makes impossible passage through public roads that constitute access to a property, hamlet, community, town, lake, sea or river shores. Without prejudice to what may be appropriate, the Judge shall order immediate opening.

##### Art. 242 Usurpation of waters

A penalty of imprisonment of six months to two years or community service of forty-five to two hundred days of two hours daily shall be imposed on whoever:

a) Diverts in his favor waters that do not correspond to him, or takes them in greater quantity than that to which he had a right; and

b) Obstructs or prevents the exercise of the rights that a third party had over said waters, in accordance with the law of the matter.

#### CHAPTER VIII DAMAGES

##### Art. 243 Damage

Whoever destroys, renders useless, causes to disappear or in any way damages movable or immovable property, totally or partially belonging to another, provided that the act does not constitute another more severely punished crime, shall be sanctioned with imprisonment of six months to two years or ninety to three hundred days fine or community service of forty-five to two hundred days of two hours daily, considering the economic condition of the victim and the amount of the damage, if it exceeds two minimum monthly salaries of the industrial sector.

##### Art. 244 Aggravated damage

Imprisonment of two to three years shall be imposed when the damage:

a) Is executed to prevent the free exercise of authority or in revenge for its decisions;

b) Is caused to archives, registries, libraries, museums or other things of scientific, artistic, cultural, historical or religious value; to property for public use, commemorative signs or monuments, tombs and other constructions in cemeteries;

c) Falls upon means or routes of communication or transit, upon bridges or canals, upon production plants or conduits for water, electricity or energy substances;

d) Is executed with violence against persons or with intimidation;

e) Leaves the victim in a serious economic situation;

f) Falls upon works, establishments or military or police installations, means of military transport or transmission, war material, supplies or other means or resources assigned to the service of the Army of Nicaragua or the National Police;

g) Produces infection or contagion in plants or animals;

h) Is perpetrated by three or more persons; or,

i) Is executed using means, procedures or substances harmful to

health or the environment.
If the damage occurs to a dwelling or residence, the penalty shall be three to five years of imprisonment.

##### Art. 245 Destruction of computer records

Whoever destroys, erases or in any way renders useless computer records, shall be punished with imprisonment of one to two years or ninety to three hundred days fine.

The penalty shall be increased from three to five years, when it involves information necessary for the provision of a public service or when it involves an official record.

##### Art. 246 Use of destructive programs

Whoever, with the intention of producing damage, acquires, distributes or puts into circulation destructive computer programs or instructions, that may cause harm to records, programs or computer equipment, shall be punished with imprisonment of one to three years and three hundred to five hundred days fine.

#### CHAPTER IX

CRIMES AGAINST COPYRIGHT AND RELATED RIGHTS

##### Art. 247 Unauthorized exercise of copyright and related rights

The following shall be sanctioned with ninety to one hundred fifty days fine or imprisonment of six months to two years and special disqualification for the same period to exercise a position, profession, trade, industry or commerce related to the criminal conduct, whoever, contravening the law of the matter, and with the purpose of obtaining an economic benefit for himself or for a third party, performs any of the following acts without written authorization from the holder of the right:

a) The translation, arrangement, or other transformation of the work;

b) The public communication of a work or phonogram by any form, means or procedure, in whole or in part;

c) The retransmission, by any wired or wireless means, of a broadcasting emission;

d) The reproduction of a greater number of copies than that established in the contract;

e) Distributing or communicating the work after the contract has ended;

f) The false attribution of authorship of a work;

g) The performance of any act that evades or attempts to evade a technological measure

implemented by the holder of the right to prevent unauthorized use of a work or phonogram;

h) The manufacture, importation, distribution and commercialization, or whoever provides mechanisms, devices, products or components, or offers installation services to evade technological measures set forth in the preceding paragraph;

i) The alteration, suppression of information on rights management; and

j) The importation, distribution, commercialization, rental or any other modality of distribution of works or phonograms whose information on rights management has been suppressed or altered.

