##### Art. 516 Destruction or appropriation of property

Whoever, on the occasion of an international or internal armed conflict, destroys or appropriates property, on a large scale and arbitrarily, without justification by military necessity, shall be punished with a sentence of four to eight years of imprisonment.

##### Art. 517

Attacks against peacekeeping mission or humanitarian assistance
Whoever, on the occasion of an international or internal armed conflict, intentionally directs attacks against personnel, installations, material, units or vehicles participating in a peacekeeping mission or humanitarian assistance mission in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations, provided that they are entitled to the protection granted to civilians or civilian objects in accordance with international humanitarian law, shall be punished with a sentence of seven to ten years of imprisonment.

##### Art. 518 Destruction of medical property and installations

Whoever, on the occasion of an international or internal armed conflict, attacks or destroys ambulances and medical transports, field hospitals and hospitals, relief supplies depots, medical convoys, property intended for assistance and relief for the civilian population and for other protected persons and against personnel using the distinctive emblems of the Geneva Conventions in accordance with international law, shall be punished with five to ten years of imprisonment.

##### Art. 519 Destruction of cultural property

Whoever, on the occasion of an international or internal armed conflict, attacks, destroys, removes, pillages, steals, misuses, commits acts of vandalism against or renders useless cultural property or places of worship, or those to which protection has been conferred by virtue of special agreements, or cultural property under enhanced protection, or buildings dedicated to education, science or charity, shall be punished with a sentence of four to eight years of imprisonment.

Whoever, on the occasion of an international or internal armed conflict, uses cultural property under enhanced protection or its immediate surroundings in support of military action shall be punished with the same sentence.

Cultural property shall be understood as movable and immovable property, such as: monuments of architecture, art or history, religious or secular, archaeological sites, monumental centers comprising a considerable number of cultural properties, works of art, manuscripts, books and other objects of historical or artistic interest, as well as buildings whose main purpose is to preserve or exhibit the cultural property described, such as museums, libraries, archives repositories and shelters intended for the protection of cultural property in case of armed conflict.

##### Art. 520 Destruction of the environment

Whoever, on the occasion of an international or internal armed conflict, launches an intentional attack that causes extensive, long-lasting and severe damage to natural resources and the natural environment, which would be manifestly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage, shall be punished with a sentence of five to ten years of imprisonment.

##### Art. 521 Prohibited weapons and methods of combat

Whoever, on the occasion of an international or internal armed conflict, uses prohibited means and methods of warfare intended to cause unnecessary suffering or loss or superfluous injury, shall be punished with a sentence of five to ten years of imprisonment.

For the purposes of this article, the prohibited means of warfare are:

a)	Poison or poisoned weapons;

b)	Asphyxiating, toxic or similar gases or any analogous liquids, materials or devices;

c)	Chemical, biological, reactive or atomic weapons;

d)	Bullets which expand or flatten easily in the human body, such as bullets with a hard jacket which does not entirely cover the interior or which has incisions;

e)	Weapons whose primary effect is to injure by fragments which cannot be detected by X-rays in the human body;

f)	Mines, booby-traps and other similar devices.

For the purposes of this article, mines, booby-traps and other similar devices are:

Mines, booby-traps and other devices equipped with a mechanism or device specifically designed to detonate the munition by the presence of commonly available mine detectors as a result of their magnetic or other influence other than direct contact during normal use in detection operations, prohibited by the Protocol on Prohibitions or Restrictions on the Use of Mines, Booby-Traps and Other Devices as amended on 3 May 1996.

Self-deactivating mines equipped with an anti-handling device designed so that this device can function after the mine can no longer do so, prohibited by the Protocol on Prohibitions or Restrictions on the Use of Mines, Booby-Traps and Other Devices as amended on 3 May 1996.

Booby-traps and other devices which are in any way attached to or associated with:

a)	Internationally recognized protective emblems, signs or signals;
b)	Sick, wounded or dead persons;
c)	Burial sites, crematoria or cemeteries;
d)	Medical facilities, equipment, supplies or transports;
e)	Toys or other portable objects or products specially designed for the feeding, health, hygiene, clothing or education of children;
f)	Food or drink;
g)	Kitchen utensils or appliances, except in military establishments, military premises or military supply depots;
h)	Objects of a clearly religious character;
i)	Historic monuments, works of art or places of worship, which constitute the cultural or spiritual heritage of peoples;
j)	Living or dead animals; prohibited by the Protocol on Prohibitions or Restrictions on the Use of Mines, Booby-Traps and Other Devices as amended on 3 May 1996;

Booby-traps or other devices in the form of apparently harmless portable objects, which are specially designed and constructed to contain explosive material, prohibited by Protocol II on Prohibitions or Restrictions on the Use of Mines, Booby-Traps and Other Devices as amended on 3 May 1996.

##### Art. 522 Responsibility of the superior

A superior who exercises authority over his subordinates, in an international or internal armed conflict, when they have been committed by forces under his effective command and control, or his effective authority and control, by reason of not having exercised appropriate control over his subordinates, shall be punished with the same sentence as that provided for the offenses described in this title, when:

a)	He had known or, by reason of the circumstances at the time, should have known that the forces or subordinates were committing those offenses or intended to commit them; and

b)	He had not adopted all necessary and reasonable measures within his power to prevent or repress their commission or to bring the matter to the attention of the competent authorities for the purposes of investigation and prosecution.

BOOK THREE ON MISDEMEANORS

### TITLE I

MISDEMEANORS AGAINST PERSONS SOLE CHAPTER

##### Art. 523 Assaults against persons

A fine of ten to forty-five days, or community service work of fifteen to forty-five days of two hours per day, shall be imposed on whoever:

a)	Causes another person an injury that does not require medical treatment beyond the first medical assistance;
b)	Mistreats or strikes another person, throws stones or similar objects at him, or in any way physically assaults him, not requiring medical treatment, or,
c)	Maliciously releases or sets a dog or other animal against a person.
